    description: "# Proposal: role-view starter pack (D2-rendered, YAML-driven, bounded customization)\n\n## Motivation\n\nHypergumbo's current outputs serve two audiences well: LLMs (the\ntoken-budgeted Markdown sketch) and queryable analysis (the\nbehavior-map JSON). There is a third audience that's underserved:\n**a human trying to perceive a codebase as a gestalt**, the way a\nhiker perceives a trail map. The full JSON is the equivalent of\nOpenStreetMap's raw XML — a queryable database, not a map. The\nsketch is closer to a map but is still list-shaped, optimized for\nLLM tokens rather than human visual perception, and not\ntask-conditioned.\n\nThe right framing: hypergumbo is structurally a **view compiler over\nthe behavior-map IR**, comparable to Sparx Enterprise Architect's\n\"one model, many materialized views\" — but with the authoring\ndirection inverted (views are derived from code, not authored by\nhumans, so they can't drift). The current CLI commands (`sketch`,\n`slice`, `routes`, `io-boundaries`, `explain`, `symbols`,\n`test-coverage`, `search`) are already view-types expressed as\ntext. What's missing is (a) **visual rendering** and (b) **explicit\ntask-conditioned view types** stationed by *role* rather than by\n*query primitive*.\n\n## Design lens: the hiking-trail-map metaphor\n\nSame terrain, role-specific projections:\n\n| Role | Hiking-map analog | Hypergumbo view |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Hiker | \"find your way\" | `onboarding` — entrypoints, public API, top-centrality modules, a guided reading path |\n| Trail maintainer | \"where's the work, what's worn out\" | `refactor-impact` — reverse-slice, dead-code, low-coverage hotspots, churn × complexity |\n| Birder | \"where's my thing happening\" | `feature-trace` — slice from a domain term, all touch sites, framework hits |\n| EMS | \"where's the danger, where are the boundaries\" | `security-audit` — I/O boundaries, taint sources/sinks, supply-chain tiers, claim verdicts |\n\nThree load-bearing observations from the metaphor:\n\n1. **Roles, not artifact-shapes, organize the starter pack.** A new\n   starter view has to clear the bar of \"this is a real role someone\n   occupies while reading the codebase,\" not just \"this is an\n   interesting query.\"\n2. **Role views vs structural views are different categories.**\n   \"Architecture\" and \"dataflow-trunk\" are projections of the\n   terrain-as-such — useful, but they're queryable references, not\n   maps-for-a-reader. The v1 starter pack is *role views*;\n   structural views are a separate concern.\n3. **Same edge, different framings.** A `calls` edge from\n   `auth.login()` → `db.query()` is a *waypoint* on the onboarding\n   view, a *boundary* on the security view, a *complexity site* on\n   the refactor view, and a *correlation anchor* on a future\n   incident-response view. Views are projections (select, annotate,\n   layout), not disjoint subsets of the IR.\n\n## Rendering: D2, emit-not-render\n\n- Emit `.d2` source files; let users render via the `d2` CLI to\n  SVG/PNG. Same \"produce text, don't render\" discipline as the\n  Markdown sketch today.\n- D2 over Mermaid: better layout engine (Dagre vs ELK) for dense\n  code-derived graphs, more expressive syntax (containers, near-of,\n  record-style nodes), nicer SVG output.\n\n## Customization: YAML view specs, thoughtfully-bounded state space\n\n\"Totally customizable within a thoughtfully-bounded state space.\"\nThe bounding is the design work — pick the axes that matter, refuse\nto expose the rest. Same pattern as hypergumbo's existing YAML\ncatalogs (`frameworks/`, `dataflow_patterns/`, `cfg_nodes/`,\n`io_primitives/`).\n\n### v1 bounded axes\n\n- **Selection (full expressivity, bounded vocabulary):** filter\n  expressions over the IR's existing well-defined enums —\n  `edge_type` (54 values per ADR-0023 registry), `Symbol.kind`,\n  `supply_chain_tier` (4 values), `access_mode`, language,\n  framework, file-path glob. Users compose filters over these\n  fields; they don't invent new fields to filter on.\n- **Landmarks (named strategies, not arbitrary predicates):**\n  `top_by_centrality: N`, `always_include: <selector>`,\n  `seeds: <symbol-ids>`. Three strategies, composable.\n- **Suppression (named knobs):** `hide_tests`,\n  `hide_below_centrality_percentile`, `hide_supply_chain_tiers:\n  [...]`, `max_nodes`. Enumerated, not predicate-shaped.\n- **Grouping (enum):** `group_by: package | file |\n  supply_chain_tier | framework | language`. Not arbitrary\n  expressions.\n- **Captions (enum templates):** `node_label: name | name_and_kind\n  | name_and_io`, `edge_label: type | type_and_line |\n  type_and_access_mode`. Pre-baked templates, not format strings.\n\n### Out of v1 bounds (explicitly)\n\n- **Visual encoding (color, shape, size, line style)** — the\n  starter pack owns these. This is the worst customization tar-pit\n  (taste, accessibility, dark mode, color-blindness). Users get the\n  views they get; if they want different colors, they fork. The\n  principle: **customize what's in the view, not what it looks\n  like.**\n- **Multi-page splitting, paging, zoom-to-fit** — defer.\n- **Layout direction / spacing tuning** — defer; the starter pack\n  picks defaults per view.\n\n### On-disk layout\n\n```\npackages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/views/\n├── onboarding.yaml\n├── refactor-impact.yaml\n├── feature-trace.yaml\n└── security-audit.yaml\n```\n\nPlus discovery from project-local + user-config paths with\nprecedence `project > user > starter`:\n\n```\n.hypergumbo/views/<name>.yaml      # project-local override\n~/.config/hypergumbo/views/<name>.yaml  # user-config (optional)\n```\n\n### CLI surface\n\n```\nhypergumbo view <name> [--entry SYMBOL] [--out file.d2]\nhypergumbo view --list\nhypergumbo view --spec ./my-view.yaml\n```\n\nOutput defaults to stdout (`.d2` source). `--out` writes to a file.\nRendering to SVG/PNG is the user's job via `d2 input.d2 output.svg`\n— documented in the README but not bundled.\n\n## v1 starter pack contents\n\nFour role views, exhaustively specified in YAML, shipped in the\npackage:\n\n1. **`onboarding`** — guides a new contributor: entrypoints + public\n   API + top-centrality modules, grouped by package, captions show\n   kind. Landmarks: `top_by_centrality: 30` ∪ all entrypoints ∪ all\n   `__init__.py` / `lib.rs` / `index.{ts,js}`.\n2. **`refactor-impact`** — for someone planning a refactor:\n   reverse-slice from a seed symbol, plus dead-code candidates and\n   low-coverage hotspots. Landmarks: seed + all transitive\n   dependents. Suppression: hide tests, hide\n   `supply_chain_tier=external|derived`.\n3. **`feature-trace`** — slice from a named entry symbol or domain\n   term (matched via `search`), showing all framework-pattern hits\n   along the way. Landmarks: seed + framework hits. Grouping:\n   `framework`.\n4. **`security-audit`** — I/O boundaries + taint sources + taint\n   sinks + supply-chain tier breakdown + claim verdicts (when\n   verify-claims has been run). Landmarks: all I/O primitives, all\n   `supply_chain_tier in [external, derived]` calls from\n   first-party code. Suppression: hide tests, hide internal pure\n   functions.\n\n## Open design questions\n\n1. **Where do role views interact with the existing CLI commands?**\n   `slice`, `routes`, `io-boundaries`, `explain` are query primitives\n   that the role views compose from. The question is whether the role\n   views are sugar over those primitives (each view internally calls\n   `slice` / `io-boundaries` / etc.) or whether they go through a\n   separate selection engine. Probably the former — reuse what\n   exists.\n2. **Caching.** Should rendered views be cached under\n   `~/.cache/hypergumbo/<repo-fp>/...` alongside the behavior map?\n   They're a deterministic function of (view spec + behavior map),\n   so the cache key is straightforward. Probably yes, but defer.\n3. **Property tests for view specs.** A view spec that produces\n   zero nodes is probably a bug. A view spec that produces >N nodes\n   (where N is a \"too big to be a map\" threshold) should warn the\n   user. Decide the thresholds.\n4. **The fifth role.** Strong candidates for a future v2 round:\n   `test-author` (where to put a regression test), `release-engineer`\n   (blast-radius of a release), `incident-responder` (paths reaching\n   an observed stack frame). Hold v1 at four; let the next set earn\n   its way in by demonstrated user need.\n5. **Structural views as a separate track.** `architecture`,\n   `module-graph`, `dataflow-trunk` are useful but they're not role\n   views. They could ship in the same v1 OR be deferred to a\n   \"structural views\" follow-up. Probably defer — keep v1 focused.\n\n## Out of scope for this item\n\n- Interactivity (zoom, click-through, cross-reference). The output\n  is a static `.d2` file. Interactive UIs are a separate, much\n  bigger project.\n- Runtime view editing (a UI for tweaking a view spec without\n  editing YAML). Out of scope; YAML edit + re-run is the\n  development loop.\n- Structural views (`architecture`, `dataflow-trunk`,\n  `module-graph`) — separate work item if pursued.\n- Visual-encoding customization (color, shape, size, line style) —\n  per \"v1 out of bounds\" above.\n- Extending CFG/DDG persistence to enable richer views — that's\n  WI-zopis (the sidecar item); independent.\n\n## Origin\n\nDiscussed 2026-05-19 in a familiarization conversation about\nhypergumbo's output model. The chain of reasoning was: the full\nbehavior-map JSON isn't human-gestalt-perceivable; the sketch is\ncloser but list-shaped and LLM-token-optimized rather than\nvisually-organized; Sparx EA's multi-view discipline points at the\nright architecture (one model, many materialized views) but with\nhypergumbo's inverted authoring direction (derived, not\nhuman-authored); the hiking-trail-map metaphor sharpens that into\n**role views** as the v1 starter pack; \"totally customizable\" is\nimprecise — the goal is \"totally customizable within a\nthoughtfully-bounded state space,\" which pushes the design work\ninto picking the right axes and holding the line on the rest."  # 265a
    fields: {}  # 265a
